Why Is Demand Increasing
AI and high-performance computing. Semiconductor Insight rates this a high-impact driver because servers require more layers and tighter high-speed routing. On 15 September 2026, TTM Technologies announced that its electronica India 2026 showcase would include high-speed PCB technology for AI and hyperscale data centers, advanced interconnect and next-generation RF solutions.
Automotive electronics. Also rated high impact, as EVs and ADAS increase electronic content per vehicle, with battery systems, radar, cameras and centralized controllers requiring multilayer boards with strong thermal and reliability performance.
5G infrastructure. Rated medium-high impact: base stations and network equipment need high-frequency multilayer PCBs, and higher frequencies increase material and signal-integrity requirements. Device miniaturization (medium impact) adds demand for HDI boards.
Fab & Technology Pulse: The Layer-Count Ladder

In multilayer PCBs, layer count is the clearest signal of where value sits. Four-to-eight-layer boards are the largest mainstream segment, serving consumer, industrial and automotive electronics, but commodity 4-6 layer boards face strong pricing pressure. Eight-to-twelve-layer boards serve more complex automotive and industrial systems, and 12+ layer boards grow in servers, networking, aerospace and high-performance computing, where routing density and signal integrity are critical. These premium boards command higher prices because process windows are tighter and customer qualification is longer. Each added layer means repeated lamination, drilling and plating, so yield loss rises quickly if alignment or via quality is poor, and higher-layer boards cut effective throughput compared with simple boards. For fabricators, the takeaway is that growth comes from climbing the ladder, not from adding commodity capacity.

Segmentation Highlights
By type: Rigid PCBs, Flexible PCBs and Rigid-Flex PCBs. Rigid boards remain the largest, while flexible and rigid-flex structures grow faster in compact electronics and vehicles.
By layer count: 4-8 Layer Boards (the largest category), 8-12 Layer Boards and 12+ Layer Boards.
By application: Consumer Electronics (the largest volume segment), Automotive Electronics, Industrial Equipment, Medical Devices and Aerospace & Defense. Medical and aerospace markets are smaller but higher value because qualification and traceability requirements are stricter.
By technology: Standard Multilayer, HDI, Integrated Passive Devices and Embedded Components, with HDI the strongest technology-growth segment.
Regional Outlook
Asia Pacific: More than 60% of global demand. China is the largest manufacturing base, while Taiwan, Japan and South Korea lead advanced HDI and high-reliability production. Southeast Asia and India are attracting diversification investment.
North America: A high-value region for AI servers, aerospace, defense and medical electronics. Manufacturing volume is lower than in Asia, but design complexity and qualification requirements support premium board pricing.
Europe: Specializes in automotive, industrial and high-reliability electronics, with Germany a major demand center and AT&S supporting advanced interconnect.
South America and Middle East & Africa: Smaller markets where advanced boards are largely imported. Brazil leads South America, while Israel brings advanced electronics design capability and Gulf projects use imported boards in networking and industrial systems.

